Zinc oxide (ZnO) is a promising electron-transport layer (ETL) in thin-film photovoltaics. However, the poor chemical compatibility between commonly used sol–gel-derived ZnO nanostructures and organo–metal halide perovskites makes it highly challenging to obtain efficient and stable perovskite solar cells (PSCs). Here, a novel approach is reported for low-temperature processed pure ZnO ETLs for planar heterojunction PSCs based on ZnO quantum dots (QDs) stabilized by dimethyl sulfoxide (DMSO) as easily removable solvent molecules. With no need for the ETL doping or surface modification, the champion PSC comprising the mixed-cation and mixedhalide $\mathsf{C s}_{5}(\mathsf{M A}_{0.17}\mathsf{F A}_{0.83})_{95}\mathsf{P b}(\mathsf{I}_{0.83}\mathsf{B r}_{0.17})_{3}$ absorber layer reaches a maximum power conversion efficiency of $20.05\%$ , which is significantly higher than that obtained for a reference device based on a standard sol–gel-derived ZnO nanostructured layer $(77.78\%)$ . Thus, along with the observed better operational stability in ambient conditions and elevated temperature, the champion device achieves the state-of-the-art performance among reported non-passivated pure $z_{n0}$ ETL-based PSCs. The improved photovoltaic performance is attributed to both a higher uniformity of the surface morphology and a lower defects density of films based on the organometallic-derived QDs that are likely to ensure the enhanced stability of the ZnO/perovskite interface.
## 1. Introduction
Perovskite solar cells (PSCs) technology has emerged as one of the most significant scientific breakthroughs in recent years and the development of PSCs is at the forefront of modern research.[1] Interest in planar PSCs has attracted increasing attention recently due to their relatively low costs, simple architecture, and low-temperature fabrication process. While achieving high power conversion efficiencies (PCE), over the past decade, the PCE has impressively risen from $3.8\%$ in the pioneering study[2] to a recent certified value of $25.7\%$ ,[3] the technology is still struggling with the challenges such as stability of the perovskite materials, interfaces and durability of PSCs.[4–7] Hence, developing both charge-selective and transport layers of high efficiency and longterm stability is highly desired to obtain high-performance PSCs.[8–12] Typically, PSCs are based on a high temperature sintered titanium dioxide $\left(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\right)$ as an electron transport layer (ETL). However, this widely employed electron transport material showcases some drawbacks regarding the inherent physicochemical properties and the layer fabrication, which requires high working temperatures to process the $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ nanoparticles.[13,14] Notably, an upand-coming alternative to polycrystalline $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ are zinc oxide $(\mathrm{{ZnO})}$ nanostructured materials as the potential ETL due to the exceptional optical transparency, suitable work function, and high electron mobility of ZnO thin films.[15] Initially, $z_{\mathrm{nO}}$ has attracted much attention as an alternative to $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ in dye-sensitized and polymer solar cells[16–18] and more recently in PSCs,[19–21] including flexible PSC devices.[22] Various nanostructures of $z_{\mathrm{nO}}$ , such as nanoparticles, nanorods, nanotubes, and quantum dots (QDs) have been explored for application in PSCs.[23,24] Notably, in the case of QDs, the injection of electrons is increased by producing multiple excitons while absorbing UV light.[25] Table S1 (Supporting Information) summarizes photovoltaic characteristics of selected planar $\mathrm{{}}Z\mathrm{{nO}}$ -based PSCs with various device architectures and the data demonstrates that the efficiency of $z\mathrm{nO}$ -based PSCs has varied in a wide range depending on multiple factors, including device architecture and the nature of $\mathrm{znO}$ films among others. Notably, the reported studies have usually applied $\mathsf{Z n O}$ nanostructured materials fabricated by sol–gel synthesis based on zinc acetate and an alkali metal hydroxide as the starting reagents. However, this standard wet-chemical approach provides nanoparticles in the quantum size regime featuring substantial imperfections in surface passivation and substantially labile surface-coating ligand interactions,[26] leading to a large number of traps.[27] Remarkably, a typical sol–gel process requires up to a few weeks of aging time for the complete removal of the insulating shell.[28,29] For example $z_{\mathrm{{nO}}}$ films processed by sol– gel at low temperatures $(<200~^{\circ}\mathrm{C})$ brings on the incomplete removal of the organic residues[30] and exhibit poor crystallinity resulting in a large number of traps at the interfaces involving $z\mathrm{nO}$ layers.[31] In turn, high-temperature (often over $450~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ negating many of the potential advantages of $\mathrm{znO}$ including its fabrication of flexible PSCs) annealing of films derived from a precursor solution[30] or nanoparticulate materials is essential to obtain pure $z_{\mathrm{nO}}$ films without organic contamination derived from applied reagents. Moreover, our recent studies connivingly demonstrated that using alkali metal hydroxide in standard sol–gel procedure inherently leads to alkali-metal contaminated $z_{\mathrm{{nO}}}$ QDs within the inorganic core and the inorganic-organic interface.[32] Strikingly, chemical instability of ZnO/perovskite interfaces has often been encountered[33–35] and to reduce the surface defects for enhancing the interfaces’ stability, achieve high-performance PSCs, and passivation of the $z\mathrm{nO}$ layer with inorganic materials (Table S1, entries 1–7, Supporting Information)[36–41] or various carbon-based (including organic compounds) additives have been developed (Table S1, entries 8–16, Supporting Information).[5,42–49] Remarkably, the state-of-the-art PCE $(22.6\%)$ among reported low-temperature processed PSCs was achieved for a device based on a sol–gel-derived $\mathrm{znO}$ layer modified by potassium chloride.[36] On the other hand, to avoid the participation of organic ligand in the synthetic process and/ or negligible organic impurities in the resulting $z_{\mathrm{{nO}}}$ films, alternative strategies for $\mathrm{znO}$ film growth by an atomic layer deposition (Table S1, entry 22, Supporting Information),[50] and a solution-processed combustion synthesis (Table S1, entry 21, Supporting Information, )[31] were developed. The latter novel combustion synthetic approach delivered PSCs of remarkable PCEs of $18.82\%$ and $19.84\%$ for $\mathrm{Cs_{0.05}F A_{0.81}M A0._{\cdot14}P b I_{2\cdot55}B r_{0.45}}$ and $\mathrm{Cs_{0.1}F A_{0.9}P b I_{3}}$ perovskite compositions, respectively (Table S1, entry 21, Supporting Information).[31]
Our comprehensive analysis of the mentioned above literature and the collected comparative data in Table S1 (Supporting Information) indicate the evident lack of rigorous research approaches for relatively rudimentary nanoparticulate $\mathrm{{znO}}$ materials used for fabrication of ETLs concerning their level of homogeneity, perfection, and/or their incomplete characterization. Thus, the impact of the results and the origin of the respective devices’ performance advantages and limitations remain ambiguous. There is no doubt that advancing the efficiency of devices requires the development of high-quality nanostructured $\mathrm{{znO}}$ materials fulfilling their potential and promising capabilities. As an alternative to the traditional sol–gel method, over the last few years, some organometallic approaches have developed[26,51] and new ones continue to emerge,[52] which provide actually trace metal impurities-free ZnO-based nanostructure. The preparation of high-quality, ultra-small, and isotropic $z\mathrm{nO}$ QDs has been recently advanced significantly due to the introduction of a highly promising onepot self-supporting organometallic (OSSOM) procedure via the controlled exposition of a $\left[\mathrm{EtZn}(\mathrm{X})\right]$ -type precursor (where $\mathbf{\boldsymbol{X}}=\mathbf{\boldsymbol{\mathsf{I}}}$ monoanionic organic ligand) solution to air, which leads to colloidal $z_{\mathrm{nO}}$ QDs coated with a highly-ordered organic shell composed of surface-anchored the X-type monoanionic ligands.[26,53–59] Notably, the OSSOM-derived QDs are concurrently highly processable, bio-safe,[54,59] and exhibit ultra long-lived electron-hole separation[55,58] due to relatively robust inorganic-organic interface, improved QD passivation, and stability. Astonishingly, while organometallic-derived $\mathrm{{znO}}$ QDs have been successfully applied in a nano-hybrid photo-system for $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ production with visible light[60] and semiconductorassisted light modulation of supramolecular assemblies[58] that only very recently we provided the first report on this type QDs, namely zwitterion-capped- $.Z\mathrm{nO}$ QDs, for the fabrication of an effective ETL for high-efficiency planar perovskite solar cells (PSCs).[61] We would like to emphasize that in the case of QDs, the injection of electrons is increased by producing multiple excitons while absorbing UV light.[62] Additionally, the deposition of QDs results in tighter, more compact $z_{\mathrm{nO}}$ film structures and subsequently supports the formation of the unconstrained perovskite microcrystalline layer.[63] It should also be noted that the first PSC incorporated low-temperature processed sol–gel-derived $z_{\mathrm{{nO}}}$ QDs (hereafter referred to as ZnO SG), i.e., the nanocrystals of size down to the confinement regime, was reported in 2014, and the prototypical $\mathrm{\mathrm{MAPbI}}_{3}.$ based solar cell achieved PCE of $15.7\%$ .[63] However, up to date, only a limited number of reports describe PSCs with ETLs fabricated from well-characterized colloidal ZnO QDs.[40,45,63] The observed power conversion efficiency (PCE) of all these low-temperature processed PSCs based on nanoparticulate $z_{\mathrm{{nO}}}$ ETLs significantly varies strongly depending on both electron and hole transporting layer’s processing as well as the perovskite composition and the device structure (for details, see Table S1, Supporting Information).
As mentioned above, the standard sol–gel procedures provide $z_{\mathrm{{nO}}}$ nanoparticles inherently contaminated by inorganic impurities, hence they are not suitable for obtaining highquality pure ZnO-based films. Moreover, the discussed wet strategies, both the sol–gel and organometallic approaches, have provided colloidal organic ligand-coated ZnO QDs prospective for various technological applications. However, the insulating nature of organic coating still may make it challenging to fabricate competitive solid-state devices based on reliable semiconductor electron transport materials.[64,65] Thus, searching for new methods to prepare easily processable, less-defective and organic ligand-free $\mathsf{Z n O}$ QDs to ensure enhanced carrier transport in QD thin films is still a crucial and demanding issue. Herein, a facile wet-organometallic method was developed to synthesize ZnO QDs (hereafter referred to as ZnO OM) capped by easily removable dimethyl sulfoxide (DMSO) molecules as an alternative superior electron transport material for high-efficiency and stable PSCs. As a new entryway to low-temperature processed high-quality $z\mathrm{nO}$ -based ETLs, we describe $i)$ the usage of colloidally stable $\mathrm{{znO}}$ QDs capped only by easily removable DMSO molecules as ink for the fabrication of compact $\mathsf{Z n O}$ film structures (Scheme 1b) and ii) the application of the resulting pure $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{ZnO}}$ films as a novel ETL for efficient and stable PSCs devoid of the additional $\mathrm{ZnO}_{I}$ perovskite interface engineering. The low-temperature solution-processed ETL based on $z\mathrm{nO}$ OM (hereafter referred to as OM ETL) exhibits better electrical properties and smoother surface morphology with reduced defects than the control $z_{\mathrm{{nO}}}$ SG-based ETL. The use of $\mathsf{Z n O}$ OM not only improves the electron extraction and stability of the $\mathrm{{ZnO}/}$ perovskite interface but also reduces the interfacial charge recombination. As a result, the champion planar PSC comprising the mixed-cation and mixed-halide $\mathrm{Cs}_{5}$ $(\mathrm{MA}_{0.17}\mathrm{FA}_{0.83})_{95}\mathrm{Pb}(\mathrm{I}_{0.83}\mathrm{Br}_{0.17})_{3}$ absorber layer yields a maximum PCE of $20.05\%$ , which is higher than that of the device based on $\mathsf{Z n O}$ SG $(17.78\%)$ . Moreover, the long-term stability of $z_{\mathrm{{nO}}}$ OM-based device is improved by maintaining $95\%$ of its original PCE, whereas the control ZnO SG-based PSC retained only $75\%$ of its initial PCE after $500\mathrm{{h}}$ storage in ambient conditions $25^{\circ}\mathrm{C}.$ , relative humidity ${\approx}35\%$ ).
## 2. Results and Discussion
## 2.1. Synthesis of ZnO QDs
Novel colloidal, inherently pure ZnO QDs capped by easily removable DMSO molecules (due to their relatively weak binding affinity to the surface) were prepared using a wet-organometallic approach relying on the air-promoted transformation of commercially available diethylzinc in a DMSO solution, i.e., a classical solvent for processing perovskite layers.[66] The resulting ZnO OM were separated from the highly turbid parent colloidal suspension using anti-solvent, viz. acetone (for details, see Experimental Section). Remarkably, the isolated QDs can be easily re-dispersed in DMSO to form a stable colloidal solution (without any irreversible aggregation or growth) that exhibits a pronounced Tyndall effect (Figure  1a,b). Thus, these observations along with the FTIR data of the as-prepared and post-annealed $z_{\mathrm{{nO}}}$ OM powders (Figure S1, Supporting Information) indicate that the resulting ligand-free QDs possess idealized crystal structures with essentially well-passivated surface. Typical scanning transmission electron microscopy (STEM) and high-resolution transmission electron microscopy (HR TEM) images of $z_{\mathrm{{nO}}}$ OM are shown in Figure  1c–f and Figure S2 (Supporting Information), and exhibit nearly spherically-shaped QDs with a mean core diameter of $4.7\pm0.8~\mathrm{nm}$ . The corresponding powder X-ray diffraction $(\mathsf{p X R D})$ pattern (Figure S3, Supporting Information) is consistent with that of model wurtzite-type $z\mathrm{nO}$ and the mean size of QDs calculated based on the Scherrer equation was $4.41\pm0.43~\mathrm{nm}$ , which is well-corroborate with the STEM data (Figure  1c–f; Figure  S2, Supporting Information). The absorption and photoluminescence spectra of ZnO OM in a DMSO solution are shown in Figure S4 (Supporting Information). The QDs revealed a broad absorption that dropped at $\lambda=330~\mathrm{{nm}}$ and a yellow emission band centered at $512\ \mathrm{nm}$ (for an excitation wavelength $\lambda_{\mathrm{ex}}=330\ \mathrm{nm}_{\mathrm{,}}$ ). Notably, the nanocrystalline character of the material along with its photoluminescence properties is preserved even after the temperature treatment, i.e., annealing at $150~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for 10 and $30\ \mathrm{\min}$ (Figure S3 and Figure S4d, Supporting Information). Moreover, organometallic-derived QDs retain relatively long-lived electron-hole separation with three lifetime components (Figure S5, Supporting Information). The major decay contribution was a fast component with a decay time of about 47 ns $(82\%)$ with second and third components around 552 ns $(14\%)$ and $2.77\upmu\mathrm{s}\ (4\%)$ (and 38 ns $(77\%)$ , 678 ns $(17\%)$ , and $3.30~\upmu\mathrm{s}~(6\%)$ for colloidal solution of $\mathrm{znO}$ OM in DMSO, respectively).
## 2.2. ZnO QDs-Based Films Fabrication and Characterization
Colloidal solutions of QDs derived from organometallic and sol–gel procedures, as representative nanosystems, were deposited on FTO substrates as thin films using the one-step spincoating route followed by post-annealing at $150~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $10~\mathrm{min}$ in ambient air. The spin coating process was repeated to achieve the $20~\mathrm{nm}$ film thickness (for details, see Experimental Section). Morphology of the resulting $\mathsf{Z n O}$ structures was analyzed using scanning electron microscopy (SEM) and atomic force microscopy (AFM) techniques. Top-view SEM images of $z_{\mathrm{{nO}}}$ SG and OM thin films reveal a dense and pinhole-free morphology consisting of granular aggregates of $\mathsf{Z n O}$ particles (Figure S6, Supporting Information). Figure S7 (Supporting Information) shows the corresponding AFM images, which are in good agreement with the SEM results in morphology and crystal size. Notably, OM sample exhibits a smoother surface with a root mean square (RMS) of $14.84~\mathrm{nm}$ than SG film $(20.86\ \mathrm{nm})$ . It is a valuable observation as the compact ETL layer with lower roughness is beneficial for improving the contact at the ETL/perovskite interface.[67]
Subsequently, the chemical composition of the resulting films was examined by X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS). Figure $2\mathrm{a}{-}\mathrm{d}$ demonstrates the comparison of binding energy states of high-resolution XPS spectra of the $\mathtt{Z n\ 2p}$ and 0 1  s core levels for films based on the ZnO SG and OM materials. The Zn 2p XPS spectra of these films demonstrate the ${\mathrm{Zn}}^{2+}$ oxidation state with peaks corresponding to $\mathrm{{Zn~}}2\mathrm{{p}}_{1/2}$ and $Z\mathrm{n}$ $2\mathrm{p}_{3/2}$ at binding energies of 1044.8 and $1021.7\ \mathrm{eV},$ , respectively. The 0 1 s XPS spectra were deconvoluted into two peaks using the Gaussian function with energies located at $529.8\pm0.1$ and $531\pm0.1\ \mathrm{eV}.$ . The peak with lower binding energy is attributed to the lattice O atoms in the wurtzite structure of $z\mathrm{nO}$ (scan A).[68] The other resolved binding energy is related to the surface defects such as oxygen vacancies and/or Zn-OH groups (scan B) on the surface of $z_{\mathrm{nO}}$ .[31,69] The higher intensity of the peak A in the OM sample compared to that in the SG sample is a signature of better $z_{\mathrm{{nO}}}$ OM-based film quality, demonstrating its defectless surface. It is also evident by the lack of a peak around $165.5\ \mathrm{eV}$ corresponding to the $\mathrm{~S~}2\mathrm{p}$ core level in the XPS spectrum of the OM sample, which indicates that DMSO molecules could be essentially removed from the $z_{\mathrm{{nO}}}$ surface during thermal annealing (Figure S8, Supporting Information) which is in line with the FTIR data of the as-prepared and post-annealed $z_{\mathrm{nO}}$ OM samples depicted in Figure S4 (Supporting Information). Furthermore, the reduced intensity of the peak B for OM film suggests that the oxygen vacancy defects and hydroxyl groups on the $z\mathrm{nO}$ surface are decreased compared to SG film. These surface defects and residual acetate ligands leftover on the sol–gel processed $z\mathrm{nO}$ surface were proven to accelerate the decomposition of the perovskite film during thermal annealing.[33] Thus, the OM film with a lower concentration of surface defects and much smoother surface morphology is expected to improve the electrical properties of the $z_{\mathrm{nO}}$ layer and the stability of the ZnO/perovskite interface (vide infra). We also note that it seems reasonable to assume that residual contaminants associated with DMSO molecules and/or their decomposition products may be still present on the $z\mathrm{nO}$ surface (further investigations on the atomic-level insight into the nanocrystals surface are in progress).
The charge transport electrical properties of ZnO OM and SG films were examined by Hall measurement and UV photo­ electron spectroscopy (UPS). As shown in Table S2 (Supporting Information), ZnO OM-based film exhibits higher electron mobility and carrier density than that observed for $z\mathrm{nO}$ SG-based film. The improvement of charge carrier mobility would reduce the effect of space charge and enhance the extraction of generated free charge carriers.[70] Thus, we expect more conductive $\mathsf{Z n O}$ OM film to be a good alternative ETL in PSCs and deliver greater photovoltaic efficiency.
The direct bandgap energy of ZnO SG and OM films was extracted from the UV–vis absorption spectra and found to be around 3.30 and $3.25\ \mathrm{eV},$ respectively (Figure S9, Supporting Information). To evaluate the band levels of both $z_{\mathrm{{nO}}}$ films, UV photoelectron spectroscopy (UPS) measurement was performed, as shown in Figures  2e,f. Figure S10 (Supporting Information)schematically shows the energy band diagrams of the investigated perovskite for both $z_{\mathrm{{nO}}}$ ETLs.[71] Using the estimated valence band from UPS results $(-7.32$ and $-7.36\ \mathrm{eV}$ for the SG and OM, respectively) and the estimated bandgap from the UV–vis test, the conduction bands (CBs) of both ETLs were calculated to be $-4.02$ and $-4.11\ \mathrm{eV}$ for $Z{\mathrm{nO~}}S G$ and OM, respectively. As seen in Figure S10 (Supporting Information), the CB of $z_{\mathrm{{nO}}}$ OM is slightly lower than that of perovskite film and there is only $20\mathrm{meV}$ band offset, which can facilitate the electron transfer from the perovskite to OM ETL.[72] Whereas for the case of SG, the CB is higher than that of the perovskite and thus there is an energy barrier $(70\ \mathrm{meV})$ for the electrons, negatively affecting the charge transfer at the interface. This can increase the interface recombination and reduce the opencircuit voltage $(V_{\mathrm{OC}})$ of the PSCs.[73,74]
2.3. Evaluating Structure, Morphology, and Optoelectronic Properties of Perovskite Films on ZnO ETLs
In this work, we employ the mixed-cation and mixed-halide formulation $\mathrm{Cs}_{5}(\mathrm{MA}_{0.17}\mathrm{FA}_{0.83})_{95}\mathrm{Pb}(\mathrm{I}_{0.83}\mathrm{Br}_{0.17})_{3}$ (further denoted as CsMAFA) as a perovskite absorber due to the high efficiency and long-term stability of the corresponding devices based on $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}[75,76]$ and $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}^{[77]}$ ETLs. Initially, the effect of the ZnO-ETL character on the perovskite layer performance was investigated and in control experiments the perovskite solution was deposited on $\mathrm{{znO}}$ SG and OM films using the spin-coating deposition method (for details, see Experimental Section). The UV–vis absorbance spectra of the perovskite layers deposited on SG and OM ETLs demonstrates similar absorption spectra over the entire visible region (Figure  3a). Remarkably, while both samples display similar pXRD patterns with diffraction peaks centered at $14.08^{\circ}$ , $28.3^{\circ}$ , and $31.7^{\circ}$ , confirming the formation of a perovskite crystal structure (Figure 3b), that the peak located at $12.7^{\circ}$ in ZnO SG/CsMAFA film indicates the presence of the $\mathrm{PbI}_{2}$ hexagonal phase likely associated with the decomposition of a small amount of perovskite during thermal annealing; no apparent peak of $\mathrm{PbI}_{2}$ can be identified in the pXRD pattern of $z_{\mathrm{{nO}}}$ OM/CsMAFA film.
The surface morphologies of ZnO SG/CsMAFA and ZnO OM/CsMAFA films are shown in Figure  3c,d. Top-view SEM image of ZnO SG/CsMAFA film reveals the less-compact perovskite morphology with several cracks and/or pinholes (as indicated by red circles). In contrast, the compact and pinhole-free perovskite film was formed on the top of OM ETL. Moreover, the perovskite film grown on ZnO OM exhibits a larger average grain size $(700\ \mathrm{nm})$ than that of the film on SG ETL $(400\ \mathrm{nm})$ ). Besides, the perovskite film deposited on OM ETL has a smaller surface roughness $\mathrm{^{\prime}R M S}=18.10\mathrm{nm}$ ) than that on SG ETL $(\mathrm{RMS}=29.71\ \mathrm{nm}$ ), providing a smoother interface in contact with the hole transporting layer (HTL) (Figure S11, Supporting Information). To further study the charge transfer process at the ETL/perovskite interface, steady-state photoluminescence (PL) and time-resolved PL (TRPL) measurements on ZnO SG/CsMAFA and ZnO OM/CsMAFA films were performed. The PL spectra show a similar emission peak position at $785~\mathrm{nm}$ for both samples, which is in good agreement with the previous report (Figure  3e).[75] Notably, the perovskite film deposited on OM ETL exhibits a significant PL quenching effect compared with that of the perovskite deposited on SG ETL or FTO substrate. The decreased PL signal indicates the reduction of the surface defects at the interface, which is wellcorroborated with the XPS data (Figure  2a). It is also in good agreement with TRPL result, which reveals similar trends to the steady-state PL spectra (Figure  3f). The extracted carrier lifetime parameters using a biexponential decay model are listed in Table S3 (Supporting Information). The shorter carrier lifetime for the perovskite deposited on OM ETL $\tau\approx75$ ns) as compared to perovskite film deposited on a glass substrate $\tau\approx108$ ns) and SG ETL ( $\tau\approx92$ ns), suggests a faster electrontransfer process due to lower interface recombination.[36,78]
## 2.4. Perovskite Solar Cell Performance and Stability
The effect of low-temperature processed pure $z_{\mathrm{nO}}$ ETLs on the performance and stability of PSCs was further investigated by fabricating a planar device consisting of FTO/ZnO/CsMAFA/ PTAA/Au. Figure S12 (Supporting Information) shows the cross-sectional SEM images of the completed PSC composed of $20~\mathrm{nm}$ thick $z_{\mathrm{nO}}$ , $400\ \mathrm{nm}$ thick perovskite absorber, $100~\mathrm{nm}$ thick PTAA, and $100\ \mathrm{nm}$ thick Au electrode. As seen, the perovskite layer deposited on $z_{\mathrm{{nO}}}$ OM ETL exhibits a better uniformity and crystallinity in compared to that film deposited on $\mathsf{Z n O}$ SG ETL. In preliminary experiments, we found that the PSCs with the $20\mathrm{nm}$ thick $z_{\mathrm{nO}}$ ETL exhibit the best photovoltaic results. Performance metrics of best devices with various thicknesses of $\mathsf{Z n O}$ ETLs, including open-circuit voltage $(\mathrm{V_{OC}})$ , short circuit current density.
$(\mathrm{J}_{\mathrm{SC}})$ , fill factor (FF), and PCE are listed in Table S4 (Supporting Information), and the representative $\mathrm{J-V}$ curves are shown in Figure S13 (Supporting Information). The device based on optimized SG ETL yields in a maximum PCE of $17.78\%$ with a $\boldsymbol{\mathrm{J}}_{\mathrm{SC}}$ of $21.95\mathrm{\mA\cm^{-2}}$ , a $\mathrm{v_{oc}}$ of $1.09\mathrm{~V~}$ and a FF of $74.34\%$ (Figure  4a). In turn, the champion device based on $z_{\mathrm{nO}}$ OM ETL exhibits superior performance with a PCE of $20.05\%$ , a $\boldsymbol{\mathrm{J}}_{\mathrm{SC}}$ of $22.93\mathrm{\mA\cm^{-2}}$ , $\mathrm{v_{oc}}$ of $1.129\mathrm{~V~}$ and
FF of $77.52\%$ , reaching the state-of-the-art performance among the reported low-temperature processed non-passivated pure $z_{\mathrm{{nO}}}$ ETL-based PSCs (Table S1, entry 21, Supporting Information).[31] The stabilized PCE output and steady-state current density of both devices at the maximum power point under continuous illumination for 250  s confirmed that the devices are stable under light over time (Figure S12, Supporting Information). The statistical data for investigated devices are shown in Figure S15 (Supporting Information), and it was found that all the photovoltaic parameters have been significantly improved for the devices based on OM ETL. Notably, the obtained PCE value for $\mathsf{Z n O}$ OM-based PSC is among the highest reported performance for pure ZnO-based PSCs without any passivating layer or doping with metal ions (Table S1, Supporting Information).[44,70] The observed increase in current density for $z\mathrm{nO}$ OM-based PSCs is verified by external quantum efficiency (EQE) measurement, as shown in Figure  4b. The integrated $\boldsymbol{\mathrm{J}}_{\mathrm{sc}}$ calculated from the solar spectrum and EQE spectra for PSCs incorporating $(\mathrm{J}_{\mathrm{SC}}=22.63\ \mathrm{mA}\ \mathrm{cm}^{-2})$ and $z\mathrm{nO}$ SG $(\mathrm{J}_{\mathrm{SC}}=21.54~\mathrm{mA}~\mathrm{cm}^{-2})$ films are consistent with the values of $\boldsymbol{\mathrm{J}}_{\mathrm{sc}}$ obtained from the $\mathrm{J-V}$ curves. Our results also show that the PSC with OM ETL has a lower hysteresis index (defined as $H I({\%})~=~[J_{b a c k w a r d}(V_{o c})~-~J_{f o r w a r d}(V_{o c})]/[J_{b a c k w a r d}(V_{o c})]~\times~100)]$ as compared to that of SG based device (Figure S16 and Table S5, Supporting Information). The significantly suppressed HI can be attributed to the improved charge transfer and reduced interface recombination likely due to a particular character of the developed pure $\mathsf{Z n O}$ QDs-based ETL. The suppressed charge recombination in OM based PSCs is also validated by measuring $\mathrm{v_{oc}}$ at various light intensities (Figure  4c). The smaller slope of $\mathrm{{K_{B}T/q}}$ for the title device $(\mathrm{n}=1.3)$ indicates the reduction of the trap-assisted charge recombination process compared to SG based device $(\mathrm{n}=1.6)$ , which is consistent with PL and TRPL results.
The better photovoltaic performance of OM-based PSCs can also be attributed to the improved stability of $\scriptstyle\mathrm{ZnO}_{I}$ perovskite interface due to lower interfacial defects that could decompose perovskite to $\mathrm{PbI}_{2}$ . To confirm it, the thermal stability of the perovskite films deposited on $Z{\mathrm{nO~}}S G$ and OM ETLs was studied. The films were annealed at $100~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ in ambient air with $\mathrm{RH}=40\%$ for $^{2\mathrm{~h~}}$ , and the color change was monitored, as shown in Figure S17a (Supporting Information). While the perovskite film deposited on SG ETL was completely yellow, the perovskite film deposited on the OM ETL remained black during the applied heating process. Moreover, $z\mathrm{nO}$ OM/CsMAFA film remained stable after exposure in the air with $\mathrm{RH}=40\%$ for $12\mathrm{~h~}$ (Figure S17b Supporting Information). This highlights the better quality of the contact interface between the OM ETL and perovskite film that could enhance the overall stability of the device. The shelf-life stability test was next conducted in a controlled atmosphere box (with RH $\approx35\text{-}40\%)$ without encapsulation and the $\mathrm{J-V}$ characteristic was measured over $500\mathrm{~h~}$ (Figure 4d). Whereas the PCE of the reference SG based device drops to $75\%$ of its initial value, the stability of the respective OM device obviously improves with maintaining $95\%$ of the original PCE after $500\mathrm{~h~}$ storage. We also investigated the thermal stability $85~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ , $\approx60\%$ RH) of the unencapsulated devices. As shown in Figure S18 (Supporting Information), the $z_{\mathrm{{nO}}}$ OM based PSC retains $54\%$ of its initial PCE value, whereas the PCE of $z_{\mathrm{nO}}$ SG device completely degrade after $250\mathrm{~h~}$ of thermal stress. This advantage in stability and efficiency makes the organometallic-processed $z\mathrm{nO}$ QDs a promising alternative to $\mathrm{znO}$ nanostructures commonly prepared via the sol–gel route in the practical application as an ETL in PSCs.
## 3. Conclusions
In conclusion, so far, perovskite solar cells based on $\mathsf{Z n O}$ ETLs have essentially relied on the use of organic or inorganic additives to passivate the surface of the semiconductor films. We demonstrated that $z_{\mathrm{{nO}}}$ QDs fabricated using an organometallic approach and coated by an easily-removable organic shell support the formation of uniform and smooth films during solution processing. The developed approach involves just using the spin-coating route followed by post-annealing at $150^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ and avoiding prolonged high-temperature heat-treatment or other often reported different pretreatment methods prior to depositing the perovskite. The introduction of the OM ETL in PSCs not only helps the formation of uniform surface morphology with a reduced amount of surface defects but also leads to excellent electron extraction ability owing to high conductivity and favorable band alignment with respect to the perovskite layer. In addition, the OM ETL improves the $\mathsf{Z n O}/$ perovskite interface stability and perovskite film quality, which consequently reduces the interfacial charge recombination and substantially enhances both VOC and FF. As a result, the optimized device demonstrated superior photovoltaic performance and long-term ambient as well as thermal stability compared to the control device fabricated on the sol–gel processed ZnO ETL. Consequently, all these results highlight the superiority of the developed one-pot-one-step organometallic approach that may open up new possibilities for the design and nanoengineering of efficient ZnO-based ETLs to tackle the instability issue of the $\mathrm{\Delta}Z\mathrm{nO}_{I}$ perovskite interface and offer a new direction for the fabrication of highly efficient PSCs.
## 4. Experimental Section
Materials: Diethylzinc was purchased from ABCR and handled under an inert gas atmosphere. Water was purified with Milli- $\cdot Q\circledast$ Integral Water Purification System for Ultrapure Water (Merck Millipore). Fluorine-doped tin oxide (FTO, $8\ \Omega\ c m^{2})$ , anhydrous ethanol $(99.9\%)$ , chlorobenzene $(95\%)$ , anhydrous isopropanol $(99.7\%)$ , anhydrous methanol $(99.8\%)$ , anhydrous toluene $(99.9\%)$ , anhydrous N,N-dimethylformamide (DMF, $99.8\%$ , dimethyl sulfoxide (DMSO, $99.9\%$ , 4-tertbutylpyridine (TBP, $96\%$ , lithium bis-(trifluoromethylsulphonyl) imide (Li-TFSI, $95\%$ ), zinc powder $(Z\mathsf{n})$ , hydrochloric acid (HCl, AR, $35-37\%)$ , zinc acetate dihydrate $(Z n(C H_{3}C O_{2})_{2}\cdot2H_{2}O$ , $99.999\%$ trace metals basis), potassium hydroxide (KOH, reagent grade, $90\%$ , flakes), lead iodide $(\mathsf{P b l}_{2},$ $99.9\%$ , lead bromide $({\mathsf{P b}}{\mathsf{B r}}_{2})$ , cesium iodide (CsI, $99.9\%$ , and poly(triarylamine) (PTAA) were purchased from EM INDEX.
CAUTION: Volatile dialkylzinc compounds can spontaneously inflame in air and these reagents should be kept and transferred under an inert atmosphere and handled with caution.
Preparation of ZnO OM: An appropriate amount of $\mathsf{E t}_{2}\mathsf{Z n}$ $(0.43~\mathsf{m L}$ , $2.37~\mathsf{m}$ in hexane, 1  mmol) was injected into DMSO $(70~\mathsf{m L})$ with the tip of the needle submerged in solution. Within minutes, the solution became turbid and the reaction mixture was stirred vigorously for $48\ h$ under ambient air conditions at room temperature. Then, the as-prepared ZnO OM were separated from the resulting colloidal suspension by adding acetone, and re-washed two times with THF and then dried under vacuum. For further processing, we dissolved ZnO OM in DMSO in $5~{\mathsf{m g}}{\cdot}{\mathsf{m l}}^{-1}$ concentration.
Preparation of ZnO SG: ZnO SG samples were synthesized according to the literature procedure.[63] $Z n(C H_{3}C O_{2})_{2}\cdot2H_{2}O$ (0.74 g) was dissolved in methanol at $(37m!)$ $65^{\circ}C$ with constant stirring. Next, KOH $(0.37\ \mathrm{g})$ is dissolved in methanol $(76~m!)$ and slowly added in the previous solution of $Z n(C H_{3}C O_{2})_{2}\cdot2H_{2}O$ at $65~^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ for 15  min and then the final solution was stirred for $2\ h$ at $60~^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ . Subsequently cooling the solution the precipitate washed with methanol. Finally, the precipitate was dispersed in an appropriate amount of $\mathfrak{n}$ -butanol $(74~\mathsf{m l})$ and chloroform $(7m\vert)$ and produced a $Z_{\mathsf{n O}}\mathsf{S G}$ solution ${[5~\mathsf{m g}\cdot\mathsf{m}]^{-1}}$ concentration).
Device Fabrication: PSCs were fabricated on precleaned FTO glass. The ZnO OM solution in DMSO $(5~\mathsf{m g}~\mathsf{m l}^{-})$ was filtered through a $0.45~\mathsf{m m}$ PVDF syringe filter before the deposition. The OM film was spin-coated on FTO at 4000  rpm for $30\mathrm{~s~}$ and annealed at ${150~^{\circ}C}$ for 10  min. The spin coating process was repeated one and two times to reach different thicknesses and finally annealed at ${150~^{\circ}C}$ for $\rceil\mathfrak{h}$ . UV-ozone treatment for 15  min was applied before perovskite layer deposition. A similar process was applied to deposit for ZnO SG films. The ZnO films thicknesses were estimated using LSE-MS profilometer. The triple cation perovskite solution was then spin-coated at 1000 and 6000  rpm for 10 and $30~\mathsf{s}$ , respectively [FAI (1  M), $\mathsf{P b l}_{2}$ (1.1  M), $\mathsf{P b B r}_{2}$ (0.2  M), MABr $(0.22~\mathsf{M})$ and CsI $(0.05~\mathsf{M})\mathsf{I}$ . Then, chlorobenzene $(\mathsf{l}00\upmu\mathsf{L})$ was dropped on the substrate 15 s prior to the end of the second steps and the film was annealed at $\mathsf{l}70^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ for $90\mathrm{\min}$ . The solution of PTAA was prepared by dissolving PTAA in toluene $(75\mathrm{\mg\7.5\ml^{-1}})$ and adding $75\upmu\up$ solution of Li-TFSI in acetonitrile $(770\ m g{\cdot}\mathsf{m l}^{-1})$ and $7.5~\upmu\up$ TBP. Finally, $80\ \mathsf{n m}$ of the gold electrode was evaporated as a counter electrode using a shadow mask. The active area of this electrode was fixed at $0.09\mathsf{c m}^{2}$ .
Device Measurement: All devices were measured under a solar simulator (AM 1.5G) with a light intensity of $\mathsf{l}00\ m\mathsf{W\ c m}^{-2}$ , calibrated by a silicon solar cell and KG5 filter. The $\rfloor_{\mathsf{s c}}$ of PSCs were verified using the external quantum efficiency (EQE) technique using a Spectral Products DK240 monochromator and by scanning the wavelength from 300 to $800~\mathsf{n m}$ .
Thin Film Characterization and Device Measurements: Powder X-ray diffraction (pXRD) pattern data for $2\theta$ values were collected with Rigaku, Japan equipped with a $\mathsf{C u k}$ line of $\mathsf{1.5470^{\circ}A}$ The field emission scanning electron microscope (FE-SEM; S-4700, Hitachi) was used to analyze surface morphology films. The atomic force microscopy (AFM, XE-100 Advanced Scanning Probe Microscope, Park System) was utilized to the measure surface roughness of the films. X-ray Photoelectron Spectroscopy (XPS Model Name: MultiLab2000) was applied to measure elements and percentage compositions. The resistivity, conductivity and charge carrier density of the ETLs were investigated using Hall measurements (Ecopia HMS-3000 Hall Measurement system). Hall measurements were performed for all thin films having an optimized thickness at a temperature of $300~\mathsf{K}$ (glass substrate $\mathsf{s i z e}=7\mathsf{c m}\times7\mathsf{c m}$ ). The transmittance or absorbance of the films were analyzed by UV–vis measurement (Model Name: Cary 300 UV–vis). The photoluminescence (PL) was measured using Acton Research Co., USA and time-resolved PL (TRPL) spectroscopy was measured using MicroTime-200, Picoquant, Germany. The UV photoelectron spectroscopy (UPS) (Model Name: Axis-Supra) was utilized to measure the band alignment of the films. For TRPL measurement, a long-pass filter (HQ500lp, AHF), a dichroic mirror (490 DCXR, AHF) was used. The pinhole for the laser was $75\upmu\mathrm{m}$ and a $700~\mathsf{n m}$ long-pass filter (FEL0700, Thorlabs) and a single-photon avalanche diode (PDM series, MPD) were employed to record the TRPL spectra. A biexponential equation (Equation  1) was used for fitting the curves.
$$
I(t)=I_{0}+A_{1}\exp\left(-\frac{t-t_{0}}{\tau_{1}}\right)+A_{2}\exp\left(-\frac{t-t_{0}}{\tau_{2}}\right)
$$
